WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.060
you need to take care of.

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.200
The first is this first line here

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:08.050
which is sort of an initialization of this loop counter

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:12.410
and the second is this line here

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:15.890
which is incrementing your loop counter.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:21.680
The reason why the second one's important

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:24.160
is because let's look at our condition here.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:26.180
So while n is less than five.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:29.740
If you didn't have this line here

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.160
you would never increment n.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.960
So every time through the loop you'd just keep

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:38.100
printing zeros okay and you'd have an infinite loop.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.680
I do want to show though what if you do have

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.500
an infinite loop it's not the end of the world.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:56.080
So I can say something like so while true print zero.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:58.860
So this is going to give me an infinite loop

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:11.030
in my program and whoop, okay.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.910
So notice it's just printing the letter p

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:17.670
over and over again and if I let it go any longer

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:18.650
it's gonna slow down the computer.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:21.450
So I'm gonna hit control C or command C maybe

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:26.150
and it's going to stop the program from printing okay.

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:28.590
So just in case you ever enter infinite loops

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:30.950
in your programs just go to the console

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:33.490
and hit control C and that's going to stop it

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:36.610
from sort of slowing down the computer.

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:40.330
Okay so going back to this example

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:41.890
I was saying that if you're using

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:45.450
counters or variables in order to sort of count up

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:47.250
inside the while loop you have to take care

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.850
to initialize a counter variable first

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:52.450
and then to increment it.

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:53.970
Otherwise you'll enter an infinite loop.

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:56.450
That feels a little bit tedious

00:01:57.310 --> 00:02:01.570
and so there's a shortcut for doing that exact same thing.

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:04.130
So these four lines you can rewrite those

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:05.790
into these two lines right here

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:08.990
using this new type of loop called a for loop.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:14.100
So the for loop says for some loop variable

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.040
in this case I named it n you can name it whatever you want.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:19.440
In range five we're going to come back

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.260
to what range means in a little bit print n.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:28.180
So every time through the loop you're going to print out

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:29.480
what the value of n is.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:34.980
Range five actually creates internally

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:37.720
a sequence of numbers starting from zero

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.220
and going to that number five minus one.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:44.060
So the sequence is going to be zero, one, two, three and four.

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:47.860
The first time through the loop you're going to say

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:50.920
n is equal to zero or internally this is what happens

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:53.560
n gets the value zero you're going to print n.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:57.440
Then you're going to go back to the top

00:02:57.440 --> 00:02:59.920
n gets the value one then you're going to go

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.920
execute whatever's inside so you're going to print one.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:05.620
Then you're going to increment that

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:07.040
to the next value in the sequence

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:09.260
you're going to print out two and so on.

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:15.840
So this is the general look of a for loop.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:19.800
So we have for some loop variable again

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:23.840
can be named whatever you want in range some number

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:28.760
do a bunch of stuff and again

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:30.720
these are part of this for loop code block

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.980
so you should indent them to tell Python

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:34.580
that these are the things that you should do.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:38.220
So when you're using range some number

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:42.840
you start out with variable getting the value zero.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:48.120
With variable having value zero

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:50.060
you're going to execute all of these expressions.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:54.280
After all the expressions in the code block are done

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:58.460
you're going to go on to the next value so one.

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:01.620
You're going to execute all these expressions

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:06.460
with variable being value one and then so on and so on

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:09.260
until you go to sum num minus one.

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:18.680
So using range in that way is a little bit constraining

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:21.980
because you're always going to get values starting from zero

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:24.640
and ending at sum num minus one.

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:27.160
Whatever's in the parentheses in range.

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:29.380
Sometimes you might want to write programs

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:31.200
that maybe start at a custom value.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:33.100
Don't start at zero maybe they start at five

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:35.020
maybe it started minus ten.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.020
And sometimes you might want to write programs

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:40.300
that don't go with don't increment

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:43.000
the numbers by one but maybe skip every other number

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.420
or go every two numbers every three numbers and so on.

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:49.680
So you can customize range to your needs.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:53.440
The one thing you do need to give it is the stop.

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:56.120
So if you give it only one value in the parentheses

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:57.800
that stands for stop.

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:00.000
And by default start is going to have the value zero
